= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Work in a <Cartesian coordinate system>, write $\partial_j=\partial/\partial x_j$, and use the <Einstein summation convention>. For a twice continuously differentiable <vector field>, the contraction of two <Levi-Civita symbols> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
[\nabla\times(\nabla\times u)]_i&=\epsilon_{ijk}\epsilon_{klm}\partial_j\partial_lu_m\\
&=(\delta_{il}\delta_{jm}-\delta_{im}\delta_{jl})\partial_j\partial_lu_m\\
&=\partial_i(\partial_ju_j)-\partial_j\partial_ju_i.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here the <Kronecker deltas> select components, and equality of mixed <partial derivatives> gives the <curl of the curl identity>
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times(\nabla\times u)=\nabla(\nabla\cdot u)-\nabla^2u.}
$$
For the second identity, the same contraction followed by the <product rule> yields
$$
\begin{aligned}
[\nabla\times(u\times v)]_i&=\partial_j(u_iv_j-u_jv_i)\\
&=u_i\partial_jv_j-v_i\partial_ju_j+v_j\partial_ju_i-u_j\partial_jv_i.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the <curl of a cross product> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times(u\times v)=u(\nabla\cdot v)-v(\nabla\cdot u)+(v\cdot\nabla)u-(u\cdot\nabla)v.}
$$
The directional <derivatives> in the last two terms act on the <vector field> immediately to their right.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a sufficiently smooth <vector field> $A$ on a volume with piecewise smooth closed boundary and outward <unit normal vector>, the <divergence theorem> states
$$
\boxed{\int_V\nabla\cdot A\,dV=\int_S A\cdot\hat n\,dS.}
$$
Expanding the <divergence of a cross product> by the <product rule> gives $\nabla\cdot(F\times G)=G\cdot(\nabla\times F)-F\cdot(\nabla\times G)$. Apply the theorem to $A=F\times G$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{\int_V[G\cdot(\nabla\times F)-F\cdot(\nabla\times G)]\,dV=\int_S(F\times G)\cdot\hat n\,dS.}
$$

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the full field in the PDF, including the exponential term missing from the transcription. Its <divergence> is
$$
\nabla\cdot F=[z\sin(yz)+3x^2]-z\sin(yz)+3y^2=3(x^2+y^2).
$$
The exponential in the third component has zero $z$ <derivative>. In <cylindrical coordinates>, the solid has $0\leq r\leq2$ and $0\leq z\leq4-r^2$. Therefore the <divergence theorem> gives the outward <flux>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_S F\cdot\hat n\,dS&=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^2\int_0^{4-r^2}3r^2\,r\,dz\,dr\,d\phi\\
&=6\pi\int_0^2(4r^3-r^5)\,dr=\boxed{32\pi}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The <integration> includes the entire closed boundary, both cap and base.

