= i
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the established time-periodic response, take a complex separated solution $v=X(x)e^{i\omega t}$ and set $u=\operatorname{Re}v$. The <diffusion equation> gives $X''=(i\omega/\nu)X$. For $\omega>0$, define the penetration depth $\delta=\sqrt{2\nu/\omega}$; the spatial roots are $\pm(1+i)/\delta$. Decay at infinity selects
$$
v(x,t)=U_0e^{-(1+i)x/\delta}e^{i\omega t}.
$$
Its real part is the solution of <Stokes' second problem>:
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=U_0e^{-x/\delta}\cos(\omega t-x/\delta),\qquad\delta=\sqrt{\frac{2\nu}{\omega}}.}
$$
The velocity amplitude decays exponentially, while the phase lags by $x/\delta$. Direct substitution verifies the <diffusion equation>, and the two <boundary conditions> follow immediately.

**These data select a unique periodic response, but do not specify an arbitrary initial transient.** With no initial velocity supplied, one may add any suitable solution of the homogeneous-boundary diffusion problem. The displayed solution is the usual established oscillatory flow after transients have decayed.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Separate variables as $u=X(x)T(t)$. The zero values at both plates give the <Fourier sine basis>
$$
X_j(x)=\sin\frac{j\pi x}{L},\qquad T_j(t)=e^{-\nu j^2\pi^2t/L^2},\qquad j\geq1.
$$
Expand the initial profile using <orthogonality>. With $s=x/L$, its coefficients are
$$
A_j=\frac2L\int_0^LU_0\left(\frac xL-\frac{x^2}{L^2}\right)\sin\frac{j\pi x}{L}\,dx=2U_0\int_0^1s(1-s)\sin(j\pi s)\,ds=\frac{4U_0[1-(-1)^j]}{j^3\pi^3}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\frac{8U_0}{\pi^3}\sum_{\substack{j\geq1\\j\ {
m odd}}}\frac{\sin(j\pi x/L)}{j^3}e^{-\nu j^2\pi^2t/L^2}.}
$$
Only odd modes occur because the initial profile is symmetric about the midpoint. The coefficients are absolutely summable, so <dominated convergence> gives $\boxed{u(x,t)\to0}$ uniformly as $t\to\infty$. The slowest decay rate is $\nu\pi^2/L^2$, associated with $j=1$.

= 3
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

**Kernel conditions.** The <clamped-beam Green function> must obey $G(0,\xi)=G_x(0,\xi)=G(1,\xi)=G_x(1,\xi)=0$. Away from $x=\xi$, $G_{xxxx}=0$. At the load point, $G,G_x,G_{xx}$ are continuous and
$$
\boxed{G_{xxx}(\xi+,\xi)-G_{xxx}(\xi-,\xi)=1.}
$$
A jump in any lower <derivative> would create a <derivative> of the <Dirac delta>, rather than the single point load required by $D_x^4G=\delta(x-\xi)$.

**Construction.** A particularly simple representation is
$$
G(x,\xi)=ax^2+bx^3+\frac{(x-\xi)_+^3}{6}.
$$
It already enforces the left-end conditions and the correct source jump. The right-end conditions give $a+b=-(1-\xi)^3/6$ and $2a+3b=-(1-\xi)^2/2$, hence
$$
a=\frac{\xi(1-\xi)^2}{2},\qquad b=-\frac{(1-\xi)^2(1+2\xi)}6.
$$
For $x<\xi$ this is already factored; for $x>\xi$, expanding and refactoring gives
$$
\boxed{G(x,\xi)=\begin{cases}\dfrac{x^2(1-\xi)^2}{6}[3\xi-(1+2\xi)x],&x\leq\xi,\\\dfrac{\xi^2(1-x)^2}{6}[3x-(1+2x)\xi],&x\geq\xi.\end{cases}}
$$
The formulas agree at the junction. The forced displacement is obtained by integrating this kernel against $f$; for continuous loads it satisfies the equation and all four end conditions. Uniqueness follows because a cubic with zero value and slope at both ends is identically zero.

**Reciprocity.** In terms of $x_<=\min(x,\xi)$ and $x_>=\max(x,\xi)$, the same expression is
$$
G(x,\xi)=\frac{x_<^2(1-x_>)^2}{6}[3x_>-(1+2x_>)x_<].
$$
It is visibly symmetric under interchange of $x$ and $\xi$. Thus the <reciprocity of the clamped-beam Green function> gives
$$
\boxed{y_1(x_2)=G(x_2,x_1)=G(x_1,x_2)=y_2(x_1).}
$$
Equivalently, two <integrations by parts> show <self-adjointness> of $D_x^4$ under the clamped conditions, with bilinear form $\int u''v''dx$.

\Image[paper-1-q3-clamped-beam-kernel.png]
{title=Point-load deflections and the symmetric Green function of a clamped beam}

