= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

With the stated negative sign in the forward exponential, the <inverse Fourier transform> is
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\widetilde f(k)e^{ikx}\,dk.}
$$
For example, this holds pointwise for <Schwartz functions>; more general square-integrable functions use the corresponding mean-square interpretation.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Differentiate under the <Fourier transform> <integral>:
$$
\frac{d^n\widetilde f}{dk^n}=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}(-ix)^nf(x)e^{-ikx}\,dx=(-i)^n\widetilde g(k).
$$
Hence the <Fourier transform of a polynomial multiple> is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde g(k)=i^n\frac{d^n\widetilde f(k)}{dk^n}.}
$$
Absolute integrability of the required moments justifies differentiation under the <integral>. The identity also extends to <tempered distributions>.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $A(k)$ be the <Fourier transform> of $e^{-x^2}$. Since $(e^{-x^2})'=-2xe^{-x^2}$, <integration by parts> gives $\mathcal F[xe^{-x^2}]=-(ik/2)A(k)$. Differentiation under the <integral> then yields $A'(k)=-(k/2)A(k)$. The Gaussian <integral> supplies $A(0)=\sqrt\pi$, so
$$
A(k)=\sqrt\pi e^{-k^2/4}.
$$
Applying part (ii) with $n=1$ gives
$$
\boxed{\mathcal F[xe^{-x^2}](k)=iA'(k)=-\frac{i\sqrt\pi}{2}k e^{-k^2/4}.}
$$
It is imaginary and odd, as expected for the transform of a real odd function.

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For sufficiently rapidly decaying functions, substitute the <inverse Fourier transform> of $g$ and interchange the <integrals>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)^*g(x)\,dx&=\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\widetilde g(k)\left[\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)^*e^{ikx}\,dx\right]dk\\
&=\boxed{\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\widetilde f(k)^*\widetilde g(k)\,dk.}
\end{aligned}
$$
The bracketed <integral> is the <complex conjugate> of $\widetilde f(k)$. This is <Parseval's identity> for the chosen normalization. One may first prove it for <Schwartz functions>, where the interchanges are justified, and then extend it to $L^2$ by the <Plancherel theorem>.

= v
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Differentiating $\int e^{-a x^2}dx=\sqrt{\pi/a}$ with respect to $a>0$ gives $\int x^2e^{-a x^2}dx=\sqrt\pi/(2a^{3/2})$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{E=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x^2e^{-2x^2}\,dx=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{4\sqrt2}.}
$$
From part (iii), $|\widetilde f(k)|^2=(\pi/4)k^2e^{-k^2/2}$. Its frequency-side energy is
$$
\frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}|\widetilde f(k)|^2dk=\frac18\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}k^2e^{-k^2/2}dk=\frac{\sqrt{2\pi}}8=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{4\sqrt2},
$$
in agreement with <Parseval's identity>.

= 5
{parent=Paper 1}
