= i
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <Hermitian matrix> satisfies $H^\dagger=H$, where $\dagger$ denotes the <conjugate transpose>. For a nonzero <eigenvector> $v$ with $Hv=\lambda v$, the scalar $v^\dagger Hv$ equals its own <complex conjugate>, and
$$
\lambda=\frac{v^\dagger Hv}{v^\dagger v}\in\mathbb R.
$$
Thus **every Hermitian <eigenvalue> is real**.

A <unitary matrix> satisfies $U^\dagger U=UU^\dagger=I$. If $Uv=\mu v$, preservation of the squared <norm> gives
$$
v^\dagger v=(Uv)^\dagger(Uv)=|\mu|^2v^\dagger v.
$$
Since $v\neq0$, $\boxed{|\mu|=1}$. These statements concern complex <eigenvectors>, even when the <matrix> entries happen to be real.

= ii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The absolutely convergent <matrix exponential> series gives
$$
U^\dagger=(e^{iH})^\dagger=e^{(iH)^\dagger}=e^{-iH}
$$
for Hermitian $H$. The exponents $iH$ and $-iH$ commute, so their exponentials multiply to the exponential of their sum:
$$
\boxed{U^\dagger U=e^{-iH}e^{iH}=I,\qquad UU^\dagger=I.}
$$
Hence $U$ is unitary. Alternatively, an <orthonormal eigenbasis> of $H$ turns the exponential <eigenvalues> into $e^{i\lambda_j}$, all on the <unit circle>.

= iii
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Taking the adjoint of $U=M+iN$ gives $U^\dagger=M-iN$. In particular, the <Hermitian parts of a unitary matrix> are uniquely determined:
$$
M=\frac{U+U^\dagger}{2},\qquad N=\frac{U-U^\dagger}{2i}.
$$
Expanding both unitary identities gives
$$
U^\dagger U=M^2+N^2+i(MN-NM)=I,\qquad UU^\dagger=M^2+N^2-i(MN-NM)=I.
$$
Subtracting shows $MN=NM$, and adding gives $M^2+N^2=I$. If $Mv=\mu v$, commutation implies $M(Nv)=\mu Nv$. The assumed simple spectrum makes the <eigenspace> one-dimensional, so $Nv=\nu v$. Thus $M,N$ have a common <orthonormal eigenbasis>.

On such a <vector>, $Uv=(\mu+i\nu)v$. If the corresponding unitary <eigenvalue> is $u=e^{i\theta}$, then
$$
\boxed{\mu=\operatorname{Re}u=\cos\theta,\qquad\nu=\operatorname{Im}u=\sin\theta.}
$$
The formulas for $M,N$ also show the same <eigenvalue> correspondence directly from $Uv=uv$ and $U^\dagger v=u^*v$. Degeneracies do not prevent existence of a common basis, although the simple-spectrum assumption makes the elementary proof immediate.

