= i
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices> supplies real <eigenvalues> and an <orthonormal eigenbasis>. Compute <eigenvectors>, normalize them, and within each repeated-eigenvalue <eigenspace> choose an orthonormal basis. Distinct <eigenspaces> are already orthogonal because symmetry gives $(\lambda-\mu)v^Tw=0$ for <eigenvectors> with <eigenvalues> $\lambda,\mu$.

Use the resulting basis <vectors> as columns of $O$. Then
$$
\boxed{O^TO=I,\qquad O^TMO=D=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n).}
$$
The order and signs of the columns can be chosen freely; rotations within a repeated-eigenvalue <eigenspace> are also allowed.

= ii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the orthogonal change of coordinates
$$
\boxed{x'=O^Tx,\qquad x=Ox'.}
$$
It preserves distances and angles and gives $x^TMx=x'^TDx'$. Thus the <quadric surface> is expressed along its <principal axes> as
$$
\boxed{\lambda_1x_1'^2+\lambda_2x_2'^2+\lambda_3x_3'^2=k.}
$$
The following three cases are the subparts of (ii) in the original PDF.

= a
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Writing the common positive <eigenvalue> as $\lambda$, the equation is $|x'|^2=k/\lambda$. Hence it is a <sphere> with
$$
\boxed{\text{centre }0,\qquad\text{radius }\sqrt{k/\lambda}.}
$$
Every orthonormal set of axes is a principal-axis set in this fully degenerate case.

= b
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\lambda_1=\lambda_2=\lambda>0$ and $\lambda_3=-\gamma<0$. Then
$$
\frac{x_1'^2+x_2'^2}{k/\lambda}-\frac{x_3'^2}{k/\gamma}=1.
$$
This is a **<one-sheet hyperboloid> of revolution** around the $x_3'$ axis. Its circular waist has radius $\sqrt{k/\lambda}$ at $x_3'=0$, and its radius grows with $|x_3'|$. In the original coordinates the axis is the <eigenvector> direction belonging to the negative <eigenvalue>.

= c
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The coordinate $x_1'$ does not occur. The perpendicular cross-section is
$$
\frac{x_2'^2}{k/\lambda_2}+\frac{x_3'^2}{k/\lambda_3}=1.
$$
Thus the surface is an **<elliptic cylinder>** extending in both directions along the zero-eigenvalue axis. Its cross-sectional semiaxes are $\sqrt{k/\lambda_2}$ and $\sqrt{k/\lambda_3}$. It becomes a circular cylinder when the two positive <eigenvalues> coincide.

= iii
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <matrix> is $M=2I-ee^T$, where $e=(1,1,1)^T$. Along $\hat e=e/\sqrt3$, its <eigenvalue> is $2-3=-1$. Every <vector> perpendicular to $e$ has <eigenvalue> two. For example, use the orthonormal basis
$$
u_1=\frac{(1,-1,0)}{\sqrt2},\qquad u_2=\frac{(1,1,-2)}{\sqrt6},\qquad u_3=\frac{(1,1,1)}{\sqrt3}.
$$
The principal-coordinate equation is
$$
2\xi^2+2\eta^2-\zeta^2=3,\qquad\frac{\xi^2+\eta^2}{3/2}-\frac{\zeta^2}{3}=1.
$$
It depends only on distance from the $u_3$ axis and position along it, so every rotation about that axis preserves the surface. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{the rotational axis is the line }x_1=x_2=x_3,\text{ with direction }(1,1,1).}
$$
This is a <one-sheet hyperboloid> with waist radius $\sqrt{3/2}$. The repeated positive <eigenvalue> makes the two transverse <principal axes> nonunique.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For real-direction increments, <complex differentiability> gives $f'=u_x+iv_x$. For imaginary-direction increments it gives $f'=(u_y+iv_y)/i=v_y-iu_y$. Equating these expressions gives the <Cauchy-Riemann equations>
$$
\boxed{u_x=v_y,\qquad u_y=-v_x.}
$$
A <holomorphic function> is smooth, so mixed <partial derivatives> commute. Differentiating these equations gives
$$
u_{xx}+u_{yy}=v_{yx}-v_{xy}=0,\qquad v_{xx}+v_{yy}=-u_{yx}+u_{xy}=0.
$$
Thus both real and imaginary parts are <harmonic functions>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla^2u=\nabla^2v=0.}
$$

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Squaring the ratio of moduli gives
$$
(x-a)^2+y^2=\lambda^2[(x+a)^2+y^2].
$$
For $\lambda\neq1$, complete the square to obtain the <Apollonius circle>
$$
\boxed{\left(x-\frac{a(1+\lambda^2)}{1-\lambda^2}\right)^2+y^2=\left(\frac{2a\lambda}{|1-\lambda^2|}\right)^2.}
$$
Its centre is $(a(1+\lambda^2)/(1-\lambda^2),0)$ and its radius is $2a\lambda/|1-\lambda^2|$. The centres are positive for $\lambda<1$ and negative for $\lambda>1$.

**At the allowed exceptional value $\lambda=1$, the locus is the line $x=0$**, the <perpendicular bisector> of the two fixed points. It is a generalized circle, but not an ordinary finite-radius circle. The point $z=-a$ is excluded from the original ratio.

= iii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For a circle in the right half-plane use $0<\lambda<1$. Its centre and radius from part (ii) satisfy $d^2-R^2=a^2$. Hence choose
$$
\boxed{a=\sqrt{d^2-R^2}.}
$$
Put $\tau_0=\operatorname{arcosh}(d/R)$. Then $\sinh\tau_0=a/R$ and
$$
\lambda=e^{-\tau_0}=\frac{d-a}{R}=\frac{R}{d+a}.
$$
Indeed the <Apollonius circle> has centre $a\coth\tau_0=d$ and radius $a/\sinh\tau_0=R$. Its logarithmic modulus is therefore $-\tau_0$, while on $x=0$ the modulus is one. The <logarithmic Dirichlet potential between a circle and a line> is
$$
\boxed{V(x,y)=\frac{V_0}{\operatorname{arcosh}(d/R)}\log\left|\frac{z-a}{z+a}\right|.}
$$
The logarithmic singularity at $a$ lies inside the excluded disk, since $d-a<R$. The other singularity, at $-a$, lies outside the right half-plane. Thus the real logarithmic modulus is harmonic throughout the required region and has exactly the two specified boundary values.

Local branches of the complex logarithm justify harmonicity; its real part is globally single-valued even though the complex logarithm need not be single-valued around the excluded disk. The displayed solution is bounded and tends to zero at infinity. A boundedness or corresponding far-field condition supplies uniqueness in this unbounded domain; finite boundary values alone do not rule out unbounded additional harmonic solutions. The same geometry appears in <cylinder-plane image charges>.

\Image[paper-1-q7-circle-line-potential.png]
{title=Apollonius circles and a bounded harmonic potential outside a circle beside a line}

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

**Singular points.** Normalizing the equation gives $p=2/x$ and $q=1-\ell(\ell+1)/x^2$. The only finite singular point is $x=0$, and it is regular because $xp$ and $x^2q$ are analytic there. At infinity put $t=1/x$ and $Y(t)=y(1/t)$; the equation becomes
$$
Y''+\left[\frac1{t^4}-\frac{\ell(\ell+1)}{t^2}\right]Y=0.
$$
The fourth-order pole makes $t=0$ an <irregular singular point>, so **infinity is irregular**.

**Exponents and recurrence.** Substitution of a series for a <Frobenius solution> gives the <indicial equation>
$$
\sigma(\sigma+1)-\ell(\ell+1)=0,\qquad\boxed{\sigma=\ell\quad\text{or}\quad\sigma=-\ell-1.}
$$
With negative-index coefficients set to zero, the coefficient equation is
$$
\boxed{[(j+\sigma)(j+\sigma+1)-\ell(\ell+1)]a_j=-a_{j-2}.}
$$
For $\sigma=\ell$, it gives $a_1=0$ and
$$
a_j=-\frac{a_{j-2}}{j(j+2\ell+1)},\qquad j\geq2.
$$
For $\sigma=-\ell-1$, it gives
$$
a_j=-\frac{a_{j-2}}{j(j-2\ell-1)},\qquad j\geq1,\quad j\neq2\ell+1.
$$
For $\ell\geq1$, $a_1=0$. At the exceptional odd index $j=2\ell+1$, the recurrence becomes $a_{2\ell-1}=0$, which is already satisfied. Thus $a_{2\ell+1}$ is free; choosing it to be zero gives an independent pure-even series times $x^{-\ell-1}$. A nonzero choice adds a multiple of the regular solution. For $\ell=0$, this freedom occurs at $j=1$ itself. This <Frobenius resonance in the spherical Bessel equation> is compatible, so no logarithmic term is needed despite the integer separation of exponents.

**Normalized regular solution.** Set $\sigma=\ell$ and $a_0=2^\ell\ell!/(2\ell+1)!$. The proposed even coefficients
$$
a_{2s}=\frac{2^\ell(-1)^s(s+\ell)!}{s!(2s+2\ell+1)!}
$$
satisfy $a_{2s}/a_{2s-2}=-1/[2s(2s+2\ell+1)]$, precisely the recurrence above. Therefore
$$
\boxed{j_\ell(x)=2^\ell x^\ell\sum_{s=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^s(s+\ell)!}{s!(2s+2\ell+1)!}x^{2s}.}
$$
This is the regular <spherical Bessel function>. The coefficient ratio tends to zero, so its series converges for every finite $x$; the other even-series factor also has infinite radius, although the prefactor has a pole at zero.

For $\ell=0$, the coefficients reduce to $(-1)^s/(2s+1)!$, giving
$$
\boxed{j_0(x)=\frac{\sin x}{x},\qquad j_0(0)=1.}
$$
Directly, $x^2j_0'=x\cos x-\sin x$, whose <derivative> is $-x\sin x$. Thus $(x^2j_0')'+x^2j_0=0$, verifying the equation. The independent singular branch at $\ell=0$ can be chosen as $\cos x/x$.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a variation $q\mapsto q+\epsilon\eta$ with $\eta(t_0)=\eta(t_1)=0$, the first variation is
$$
\delta S=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}(L_q\eta+L_{\dot q}\dot\eta)dt=[L_{\dot q}\eta]_{t_0}^{t_1}+\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\left(L_q-\frac d{dt}L_{\dot q}\right)\eta\,dt.
$$
The endpoint term vanishes. Since the interior variation is arbitrary, the <fundamental lemma of the calculus of variations> gives the <Euler-Lagrange equation>
$$
\boxed{\frac d{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q}-\frac{\partial L}{\partial q}=0.}
$$
Along such a trajectory,
$$
\frac d{dt}\left(\dot qL_{\dot q}-L\right)=\dot q\left(\frac d{dt}L_{\dot q}-L_q\right)-L_t=-L_t.
$$
If $L$ has no explicit time dependence, the first <integral> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\dot q\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q}-L=\text{constant}.}
$$
Its negative is the equivalent Beltrami form. For the usual time-independent mechanical <Lagrangian> $L=T-V$ with quadratic velocity dependence, this conserved quantity is the total <mechanical energy> $T+V$.

= ii
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the angle orientation in the PDF: $M$ has coordinates $(R\sin\theta,-R\cos\theta)$ relative to the hoop centre. The wrapped string segment has length $R(\pi/2-\theta)$. As $\theta$ increases, it releases length $R\theta$, so the suspended mass moves *down* by $R\theta$. Both speeds have magnitude $R|\dot\theta|$.

Thus the <hoop with a suspended counterweight> has
$$
T=\frac12(M+m)R^2\dot\theta^2,\qquad V=-MgR\cos\theta-mgR\theta+\text{constant},
$$
and $L=T-V$. The <Euler-Lagrange equation> gives
$$
\boxed{(M+m)R\ddot\theta=g(m-M\sin\theta).}
$$
At equilibrium,
$$
\boxed{\theta_0=\arcsin(m/M),\qquad0<\theta_0<\pi/2.}
$$
The positive curvature $V''(\theta_0)=MgR\cos\theta_0$ establishes stability. Put $\theta=\theta_0+\delta$ and linearize. The equilibrium force cancels, leaving
$$
\ddot\delta+\omega^2\delta=0,\qquad\omega^2=\frac{Mg\cos\theta_0}{(M+m)R}=\frac gR\sqrt{\frac{M-m}{M+m}}.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\omega=\left(\frac{M-m}{M+m}\right)^{1/4}\sqrt{\frac gR}.}
$$
For $M\gg m$, $\theta_0\simeq m/M$ and $\omega\to\sqrt{g/R}$, the small-oscillation frequency of a <simple pendulum> of length $R$. The <linearization> assumes an amplitude small enough to remain inside the stated angular range and a taut string throughout.

\Image[paper-1-q9-hoop-counterweight.png]
{title=Independent hoop-and-counterweight geometry and the stable minimum of its potential energy}

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write $N[\psi]=\int_a^b(p\psi'^2+q\psi^2)dx$ and $D[\psi]=\int_a^bw\psi^2dx>0$. For a real variation, with $\Lambda=N/D$,
$$
\delta\Lambda=\frac2D\left\{[p\psi'\delta\psi]_a^b+\int_a^b[-(p\psi')'+q\psi-\Lambda w\psi]\delta\psi\,dx\right\}.
$$
Suitable endpoint conditions are homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions>, homogeneous <Neumann boundary conditions> $p\psi'=0$, or one of each. They remove the variation boundary term and the product $[p\psi\psi']_a^b$. Stationarity for all interior variations is equivalent to
$$
\boxed{-(p\psi')'+q\psi=\Lambda w\psi.}
$$
Conversely, multiplication of an eigenfunction equation by $\psi$ and <integration by parts> gives $N=\lambda D$, hence $\boxed{\Lambda[\psi]=\lambda}$. General Robin data require their boundary-energy contributions unless these endpoint products happen to vanish.

Use an eigenbasis orthonormal with respect to the weight: $\int_a^bw\psi_i^*\psi_jdx=\delta_{ij}$. For complex coefficients use the Hermitian version of the quotient, with absolute squares. Self-adjointness gives
$$
\boxed{\Lambda[\widetilde\psi]=\frac{\sum_{i\geq0}|c_i|^2\lambda_i}{\sum_{i\geq0}|c_i|^2}\geq\lambda_0.}
$$
When $c_0\neq0$, dividing numerator and denominator by $|c_0|^2$ yields
$$
\boxed{\widetilde\lambda=\frac{\lambda_0+\sum_{i\geq1}|a_i|^2\lambda_i}{1+\sum_{i\geq1}|a_i|^2},\qquad a_i=c_i/c_0.}
$$
If $c_0=0$, these ratios are undefined, but the unscaled formula and upper-bound conclusion remain valid. The assumptions are a problem with a <self-adjoint operator> bounded below and a complete eigenbasis, with the trial function in its energy-form domain. The <Rayleigh-Ritz method> minimizes this quotient over an admissible trial family, obtaining an upper bound on $\lambda_0$. Higher eigenfunctions are stationary directions and need not be unrestricted extrema.

= ii
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <boundary conditions> on the positive half-line, absent from the transcription, select the <half-harmonic oscillator>. The trial state $xe^{-\alpha x}$ with $\alpha>0$ satisfies both endpoints and has finite <norm> and energy. <Integration by parts>, with vanishing endpoint term, gives
$$
\Lambda(\alpha)=\frac{\int_0^\infty[|\widetilde\psi'|^2+x^2|\widetilde\psi|^2]dx}{\int_0^\infty|\widetilde\psi|^2dx}.
$$
Using $\int_0^\infty x^ne^{-2\alpha x}dx=n!/(2\alpha)^{n+1}$, the three required <integrals> are
$$
\int_0^\infty\widetilde\psi^2dx=\frac1{4\alpha^3},\qquad\int_0^\infty(\widetilde\psi')^2dx=\frac1{4\alpha},\qquad\int_0^\infty x^2\widetilde\psi^2dx=\frac3{4\alpha^5}.
$$
Thus the <exponential trial state for a half-harmonic oscillator> gives $\Lambda(\alpha)=\alpha^2+3/\alpha^2$. Its <derivative> vanishes at $\alpha^4=3$, and the <arithmetic-geometric mean inequality> confirms the global minimum:
$$
\boxed{\alpha_*=3^{1/4},\qquad\lambda_0\leq\lambda_{\rm estimate}=2\sqrt3\simeq3.46410.}
$$
For comparison, the exact lowest half-line state is proportional to $xe^{-x^2/2}$ and has <eigenvalue> three. The exponential trial family gives a valid upper bound rather than an exact <eigenvalue>.
